  </semantics>
</math></span><img src="https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/a9885ea697e3b7320df2bfcb3b23e17869090a66" class="mwe-math-fallback-image-display mw-invert skin-invert" aria-hidden="true" style="vertical-align: -1.171ex; width:59.238ex; height:3.343ex;" alt="{\displaystyle \cdots \to H_{i}^{BM}(F)\to H_{i}^{BM}(X)\to H_{i}^{BM}(U)\to H_{i-1}^{BM}(F)\to \cdots }"/></span></p>

<p id="mwoA">Borel−Moore homology is <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Homotopy_invariant" title="Homotopy invariant" class="mw-redirect" id="mwoQ">homotopy invariant</a> in the sense that for any space <i id="mwog">X</i>, there is an isomorphism <span class="mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline" typeof="mw:Extension/math" about="#mwt31" id="mwow" data-mw='{"name":"math","attrs":{},"body":{"extsrc":"H_i^{BM}(X) \\to H_{i+1}^{BM}(X\\times \\R)."}}'><span class="mwe-math-mathml-inline mwe-math-mathml-a11y" style="display: none;"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="{\displaystyle H_{i}^{BM}(X)\to H_{i+1}^{BM}(X\times \mathbb {R} ).}">
  <semantics>
    <mrow class="MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
      <mstyle displaystyle="true" scriptlevel="0">
        <msubsup>
          <mi>H</mi>
          <mrow class="MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
            <mi>i</mi>
          </mrow>
          <mrow class="MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
            <mi>B</mi>
            <mi>M</mi>
          </mrow>
        </msubsup>
        <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
        <mi>X</mi>
        <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
        <mo stretchy="false">→<!-- → --></mo>
        <msubsup>
          <mi>H</mi>
          <mrow class="MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
            <mi>i</mi>
            <mo>+</mo>
            <mn>1</mn>
          </mrow>
          <mrow class="MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
            <mi>B</mi>
            <mi>M</mi>
          </mrow>
        </msubsup>
        <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
        <mi>X</mi>
        <mo>×<!-- × --></mo>
        <mrow class="MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
          <mi mathvariant="double-struck">R</mi>
        </mrow>
        <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
        <mo>.</mo>
      </mstyle>
    </mrow>
    <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\displaystyle H_{i}^{BM}(X)\to H_{i+1}^{BM}(X\times \mathbb {R} ).}</annotation>
  </semantics>
</math></span><img src="https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/e6d6ebac4b1dbfa2132c2083898f9503d0b0ce12" class="mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert" aria-hidden="true" style="vertical-align: -1.171ex; width:26.978ex; height:3.343ex;" alt="{\displaystyle H_{i}^{BM}(X)\to H_{i+1}^{BM}(X\times \mathbb {R} ).}"/></span> The shift in dimension means that Borel−Moore homology is not homotopy invariant in the naive sense. For example, the Borel−Moore homology of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euclidean_space" title="Euclidean space" id="mwpA">Euclidean space</a> <span class="mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline" typeof="mw:Extension/math" about="#mwt32" id="mwpQ" data-mw='{"name":"math","attrs":{},"body":{"extsrc":"\\R^n"}}'><span class="mwe-math-mathml-inline mwe-math-mathml-a11y" style="display: none;"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="{\displaystyle \mathbb {R} ^{n}}">
  <semantics>
    <mrow class="MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
      <mstyle displaystyle="true" scriptlevel="0">
        <msup>
          <mrow class="MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
            <mi mathvariant="double-struck">R</mi>
          </mrow>
          <mrow class="MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
            <mi>n</mi>
          </mrow>
        </msup>
      </mstyle>
    </mrow>
    <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\displaystyle \mathbb {R} ^{n}}</annotation>
  </semantics>
</math></span><img src="https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/c510b63578322050121fe966f2e5770bea43308d" class="mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert" aria-hidden="true" style="vertical-align: -0.338ex; width:2.897ex; height:2.343ex;" alt="{\displaystyle \mathbb {R} ^{n}}"/></span> is isomorphic to <span class="mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline" typeof="mw:Extension/math" about="#mwt33" id="mwpg" data-mw='{"name":"math","attrs":{},"body":{"extsrc":"\\Z"}}'><span class="mwe-math-mathml-inline mwe-math-mathml-a11y" style="display: none;"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="{\displaystyle \mathbb {Z} }">
  <semantics>
    <mrow class="MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
      <mstyle displaystyle="true" scriptlevel="0">
        <mrow class="MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
          <mi mathvariant="double-struck">Z</mi>
        </mrow>
      </mstyle>
    </mrow>
    <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\displaystyle \mathbb {Z} }</annotation>
  </semantics>
</math></span><img src="https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/449494a083e0a1fda2b61c62b2f09b6bee4633dc" class="mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert" aria-hidden="true" style="vertical-align: -0.338ex; width:1.55ex; height:2.176ex;" alt="{\displaystyle \mathbb {Z} }"/></span> in degree <i id="mwpw">n</i> and is otherwise zero.</p>

<p id="mwqA"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poincaré_duality" title="Poincaré duality" id="mwqQ">Poincaré duality</a> extends to non-compact manifolds using Borel–Moore homology. Namely, for an oriented <i id="mwqg">n</i>-manifold <i id="mwqw">X</i>, Poincaré duality is an isomorphism from singular cohomology to Borel−Moore homology,
<span class="mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-block" typeof="mw:Extension/math" about="#mwt34" id="mwrA" data-mw='{"name":"math","attrs":{"display":"block"},"body":{"extsrc":"H^i(X) \\stackrel{\\cong}{\\to} H_{n-i}^{BM}(X)"}}'><span class="mwe-math-mathml-display mwe-math-mathml-a11y" style="display: none;"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block" alttext="{\displaystyle H^{i}(X){\stackrel {\cong }{\to }}H_{n-i}^{BM}(X)}">
  <semantics>
    <mrow class="MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
      <mstyle displaystyle="true" scriptlevel="0">
        <msup>
          <mi>H</mi>
          <mrow class="MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
            <mi>i</mi>
          </mrow>
        </msup>
        <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
        <mi>X</mi>
